cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Cite the software version used and the associated RCC paper."
title: "RCC RefCert: A Research Toolkit for Structure-Fair Quantum Circuit Complexity"
type: software
version: 0.4.0
url: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert"
abstract: >-
  RCC RefCert is an open-source toolkit for studying quantum circuit
  complexity. Given a preparation model and final-state data, it computes a
  rigorous lower bound on optimal quantum circuit complexity. The inputs can
  be an exact target spectrum or finite measurement data collected under a
  prespecified protocol. The toolkit implements the Reference-Contingent
  Complexity (RCC) framework of Liu, Tian and Wu.
  Structural fairness fixes the background resources, permitted controls and
  their accounting before the target is chosen. A terminal-state certificate
  bounds the cost of every circuit that prepares the target to the stated
  accuracy. Researchers can build finite-control quantum models, verify
  reference certificates and compare candidate preparations. Exact
  diagonal-preserving models support two-sided cost intervals. General matrix
  models provide numerical candidate diagnostics. Window RCC resolves how
  complexity is distributed across the subsystems of pure and mixed states
  at a common reference and control bandwidth. Researchers can plan a
  measurement for a chosen cost threshold, compare measured-window RCC
  from outcome counts, and share the inputs for independent recomputation.
keywords:
  - quantum circuit complexity
  - structure-fairness
  - reference-contingent complexity
  - quantum information
